 the participants that it's  framed as being a research activity and  that as citizen scientists you know  they're co they're collaborating in this  research activity  there's very little supervision or  training that happens and you kind of  let the process  unfold the interesting parts of it i  think are that  you give three varieties per farmer  these are blind so  the the farmers don't know which  varieties they are and they only rank  for best and worse  at overall or each time point however  you choose and each farm is an  incomplete block so we had up to 30 to  50 reps  across different farmers for different  varieties depending on how you design  it and you have a sequentially balanced  design so that you make sure that you  have replication  across different regions for example  depending on different product uses in  different regions  and the bottom line is you really  embrace variation in the environment and  crop management  don't try to control for it  so if you're interested there's a paper  from 2019 from yuco buenos team  and pnas and they really showed how the  method played out for beans in costa  rica  for durum wheat in ethiopia and for  bread wheat in india looking at how  different varieties of different colors  or different  varieties how they were performing  across the different regions  for these three different crops  so again we try to do things a little  bit differently with tricot so  talking to yucca we try to understand  first of all  how participants were selected and if we  can do that a little bit differently  so instead of relying on village leads  or lead farmers to kind of recruit  participants we made sure that we did a  lot of groundwork through interviews and  focus groups to understand the diversity  in each of the communities that we  worked in  so we didn't only have geographically  representative sampling but we also  looked at the differences in wealth sex  age migration status religion within  each community so that  t